  "slug": "arithmetic-series-oq-00-oq-02",
  "title": "Can we prove the weighted generalization: ∑_{k=1}^n (2k-1)·k² = (∑_{k=1}^n k)²",
  "phase": "COMPLETED",
  "status": "completed",
  "tier": "A",
  "path": "full",
  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "The conjecture sum_{k=1}^n (2k-1)k^2 = (sum_{k=1}^n k)^2 is false: n=2 gives 13 != 9; the corrected closed form is 6 * sum_{k<n} (2(k+1)-1)(k+1)^2 + n(n+1) = n^2(n+1)(3n+1).",
    "plain": "The weighted generalization is false after n=1: n=2 is already a counterexample. The Lean development proves counterexamples and the corrected closed form.",
    "whyMatters": [
      "Separates the true Nicomachus sum-of-cubes identity from a plausible but false odd-weighted variant.",
      "Records the corrected closed form so later agents do not re-open a disproved conjecture."
    ]
  },
